<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<p>A roof settlement depends on more than the damage itself:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deductibles.</strong> Wind/hail deductibles are often a percentage of the dwelling limit; named-storm deductibles may also apply. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> On replacement cost policies, insurers typically pay ACV first and release recoverable depreciation after the roof is replaced. ACV-only roof endorsements and roof payment schedules can reduce what you receive.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> McAllen adopted the 2024 International Residential Code and International Building Code effective January 1, 2026. Code-required items beyond the original construction are paid only to the extent your policy covers ordinance or law. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes</a> and <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-mcallen/">how building codes affect insurance repairs</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits.</strong> Detached garages, carports, and sheds fall under other structures coverage with a separate limit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster in McAllen works for the policyholder. On a roof claim we may inspect and measure the roof, document damage slope by slope, compare the carrier&rsquo;s scope with what the repair actually requires, and present a supplement when items are missing. If the insurer has already paid, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">estimate review</a> can identify gaps; if the claim is underpaid, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>. Under Texas law, a public adjuster may not take part in repairing a property on a claim they adjust, so we do not act as your roofer. We cannot promise a particular result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the damage promptly and keep a record of your claim number and contacts.</li>
<li>Protect the property from further damage with tarps or temporary repairs, and save receipts.</li>
<li>Do not discard damaged roofing materials before the insurer has had a chance to inspect.</li>
<li>Provide a sworn proof of loss when requested and within the policy&rsquo;s timeframe.</li>
<li>Allow reasonable inspections and respond to document requests.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Roof claims are often limited by exclusions for wear and tear, deterioration, faulty workmanship, and long-term leaks; cosmetic damage endorsements on metal roofs; ACV-only roof coverage or payment schedules; and deadlines to complete repairs before recoverable depreciation is released. Policies may also set a deadline to file suit. For legal questions about deadlines or disputes, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
